@page "/help"

<div class="page-header"><h1>Help</h1></div>

@* Help for each page, the cron syntax and how to extend the scheduler (FR-54). *@
<div class="split">
    <nav class="list-menu no-print" aria-label="Help topics">
        @foreach (var (key, title) in Topics)
        {
            <button class="btn @(_topic == key ? "selected" : "")" @onclick="() => _topic = key">@title</button>
        }
    </nav>
    <div class="card">
        @switch (_topic)
        {
            case "start":
                <h2>Getting started</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li>Open <a href="tasks">Tasks</a> and select <strong>New task</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Give the task a unique name and choose a <strong>task type</strong>. The type decides what the task does; modules add more types.</li>
                    <li>Choose when it runs: on a cron schedule, every interval, or once. The <strong>Next runs</strong> list shows the next five times before you save.</li>
                    <li>Optionally fill in the payload (settings for the task type; use the example link), priority, retries and time-out.</li>
                    <li>Save. The engine picks the task up straight away. Use <strong>Run now</strong> to test it, then check <a href="executions">Execution history</a>.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>Fields marked with a red star are required. If a value is not accepted, the reason is shown beneath the field.</p>
                break;
            case "pages":
                <h2>The pages</h2>
                <dl class="kv">
                    <dt>Dashboard</dt><dd>Engine status, the next run, counts for the last 24 hours, charts and the latest runs. It updates by itself.</dd>
                    <dt>Tasks</dt><dd>Search, filter and sort tasks; run, pause, resume, delete and restore them. Select a name to edit it.</dd>
                    <dt>Queue</dt><dd>Runs that are due and waiting for a worker, and runs a worker has claimed. Waiting runs can be cancelled.</dd>
                    <dt>Execution history</dt><dd>Every run with its status, timing and host. Select a row for the output, error and log. Export to CSV.</dd>
                    <dt>Error log</dt><dd>Failed and timed-out runs with their error messages and technical details.</dd>
                    <dt>Reports</dt><dd>Five reports for a date range: summary, failures, performance, upcoming schedule and sign-ins. Export or print.</dd>
                    <dt>Configuration</dt><dd>Scheduler settings with their allowed ranges, per-task retry overrides and logging providers. Administrators only.</dd>
                    <dt>Modules</dt><dd>The loaded modules and the task types each one provides.</dd>
                    <dt>Backup and restore</dt><dd>Make a backup now, see every backup with its check result, and restore one. Administrators only.</dd>
                </dl>
                break;
            case "status":
                <h2>Statuses</h2>
                <h3>Tasks</h3>
                <p><StatusBadge Value="ScheduledTaskStatus.Active" /> runs on its schedule. <StatusBadge Value="ScheduledTaskStatus.Paused" /> does not run on
                    its schedule but can be run manually with <strong>Run now</strong>; that run keeps its retries. <StatusBadge Value="ScheduledTaskStatus.Disabled" />
                    does not run at all, not even with Run now, until it is enabled.</p>
                <table class="table">
                    <thead><tr><th>From</th><th>Action</th><th>To</th><th>Effect on the queue and running work</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>(new)</td><td>Create</td><td>Active</td><td>The next run is calculated.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Active</td><td>Pause</td><td>Paused</td><td>No scheduled runs. Waiting runs are cancelled when a worker picks them up. A running run finishes.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Paused</td><td>Resume</td><td>Active</td><td>The next run is recalculated and the misfire policy applies.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Active or Paused</td><td>Disable</td><td>Disabled</td><td>Nothing runs and Run now is blocked. Waiting runs are cancelled. A running run finishes.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Disabled</td><td>Enable</td><td>Active</td><td>As Resume.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Any</td><td>Delete</td><td>(deleted)</td><td>Hidden from the list. Waiting runs are cancelled. The history is kept.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deleted</td><td>Restore</td><td>Previous status</td><td>As Resume.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Paused</td><td>Run now</td><td>Stays Paused</td><td>One manual run is queued and executed, with its retries.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Only administrators can change a task's status.</p>
                <h3>Priority and order</h3>
                <p>Priority runs from 1 (highest) to 10 (lowest). When more runs are due than there are free workers, the run with the highest
                    priority starts first; runs of equal priority start in order of their due time, then in the order they were queued.
                    MaxConcurrency (1 to 16, default 4) is the number of runs that can execute at the same time.</p>
                <h3>Runs after (dependencies)</h3>
                <p>A task that runs after other tasks is queued once <strong>all</strong> of them have succeeded since its last dependency run.
                    If one of them fails with no retries left, the task is not queued for that round; it waits until that task succeeds again.
                    Only one dependency run of a task waits in the queue at a time.</p>
                <h3>Runs</h3>
                <p><StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.Running" /> is in progress. <StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.Success" /> finished.
                    <StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.Retrying" /> failed and a retry is queued. <StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.Failed" /> failed with no retries left.
                    <StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.TimedOut" /> took longer than its time-out. <StatusBadge Value="ExecutionStatus.Cancelled" /> was stopped, for example
                    when the application closed; it is queued again.</p>
                <h3>Retries</h3>
                <p>A failed or timed-out run is retried up to the task's retry limit (or the global MaxRetryLimit). The wait starts at
                    RetryBaseDelaySeconds and doubles each time, up to RetryMaxDelaySeconds.</p>
                break;
            case "cron":
                <h2>Cron expressions</h2>
                <p>Five fields separated by spaces: <code>minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week</code>. Times are in the task's time zone.</p>
                <table class="table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Allowed</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Minute</td><td>0–59</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Hour</td><td>0–23</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Day of month</td><td>1–31</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Month</td><td>1–12 or JAN–DEC</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Day of week</td><td>0–6 (0 = Sunday) or SUN–SAT</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p><code>*</code> any value · <code>5,10</code> a list · <code>1-5</code> a range · <code>*/15</code> every 15th value.
                    When both day fields are restricted, a day matching either one runs; a day field that starts with <code>*</code>
                    (such as <code>*/2</code>) counts as unrestricted for this rule, so both fields must then match.</p>
                <p>When the clocks go forward, a run time that does not exist that night (for example 02:30 when 02:00 becomes 03:00)
                    runs at the first valid minute after the change. When the clocks go back, a repeated time runs once.</p>
                <table class="table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td class="mono">*/5 * * * *</td><td>Every 5 minutes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="mono">0 * * * *</td><td>Every hour, on the hour</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="mono">0 2 * * *</td><td>Every day at 02:00</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="mono">30 8 * * 1-5</td><td>Weekdays at 08:30</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="mono">0 9 1 * *</td><td>The first of every month at 09:00</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="mono">0 18 * * FRI</td><td>Fridays at 18:00</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>When the clocks change, a time that does not exist that day is skipped, and a time that happens twice runs once. Use UTC for tasks that must run at exact intervals.</p>
                break;
            case "missed":
                <h2>Missed runs</h2>
                <p>If the computer was off or the scheduler was closed when runs were due, the task's <strong>If runs were missed</strong> setting decides what happens when it starts again:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Run once to catch up</strong> (default): one run now, then the normal schedule.</li>
                    <li><strong>Skip them</strong>: no catch-up run; the next scheduled time is used.</li>
                    <li><strong>Run every missed run</strong>: each missed run is queued, up to the RunAllMisfireCap setting.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>A run that was in progress when the application closed is cancelled and queued again, so no work is lost.</p>
                break;
            case "extend":
                <h2>Extending the scheduler</h2>
                <p>New behaviour is added as a <strong>module</strong>, without changing the core projects.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Create a class library that references <span class="mono">Scheduler.Extensibility</span> only.</li>
                    <li>Write a task handler: a class implementing <span class="mono">ITaskHandler</span> with a unique <span class="mono">TaskType</span>
                        (for example <span class="mono">MyCompany.SendReport</span>), a display name, an optional payload example, and
                        <span class="mono">ExecuteAsync</span>, which returns a <span class="mono">TaskResult</span>. Use the cancellation token for time-outs.</li>
                    <li>Write a module: a class implementing <span class="mono">ISchedulerModule</span> whose <span class="mono">Register</span> method adds the
                        handler (<span class="mono">services.AddTransient&lt;ITaskHandler, MyHandler&gt;()</span>) and any services. It may add pages (Razor
                        components with an <span class="mono">@@page</span> route) and menu entries, and create its own tasks in <span class="mono">InitializeAsync</span>.</li>
                    <li>Add the module to <span class="mono">ModuleCatalog.cs</span> in the Admin project and add a project reference.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>To remove a module, delete its line in ModuleCatalog.cs and its project reference. Its task types, pages and menu entries disappear;
                    existing tasks of its types stay in the list but are not run on this host.</p>
                <p>The Login and Security module is itself a module: remove it to run without sign-in, or replace <span class="mono">IAuthenticationProvider</span>
                    to sign in against another directory. The Inventory Monitor and Maintenance Agent projects are worked examples.</p>
                break;
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly (string Key, string Title)[] Topics =
    [
        ("start", "Getting started"),
        ("pages", "The pages"),
        ("status", "Statuses and retries"),
        ("cron", "Cron expressions"),
        ("missed", "Missed runs"),
        ("extend", "Extending the scheduler")
    ];

    private string _topic = "start";
}
